From: Carcara core-Alethe evaluation
Subject: [PATCH] do not build one-argument and/or applications

`simplify_neutral_proof` and `simplify_ACidem_proof` build their result with
`DAG_new_stack`/`DAG_new` with no case for a single surviving argument, so
removing the last neutral element from `(or a false)` yields `(or a)`. The next
stage, `simplify_or_proof`, has an arity-1 branch that collapses it back and
emits an `or_simplify` step for it -- so the one-argument application, which is
not well-formed Alethe, is written into the proof both as a `:named` term and as
that step's left-hand side.

Over the SMT-LIB corpus used for the core-Alethe evaluation this produces 1666
singleton applications (1483 `or`, 183 `and`) across 71 of 489 proofs, and
exactly 1666 `and`/`or_simplify` steps that collapse one -- a one-to-one
correspondence, so none of them is ever used for anything else.

When a single argument survives, it *is* the result. The guard idiom is already
used elsewhere in the codebase, e.g. `instantiation/inst-pre.c:519`. The driver
in `simplify.c` already copes with a collapsed result: after the neutral stage it
does `if (DAG_symb(new_src) != CONNECTOR_OR) continue;`, which re-enters the loop
on the new node. The arity-1 branches of `simplify_and_proof`/`simplify_or_proof`
become dead code.

Tested: rebuilt veriT 2026.05 and regenerated the 71 affected proofs with
`--proof-with-sharing --proof-prune --proof-merge`. All 71 still solve, no
singleton applications remain, and the proofs are 1666 steps shorter.

Note for consumers: collapsing in the first stage turns two steps into one,
`(= (or false B) B)`. When `B` is itself a disjunction, a checker that reads the
right-hand side of `or_simplify` as the *list* of surviving arguments will see
two survivors rather than one. Carcara needed a one-line relaxation to accept
both readings; other consumers may too.
---
--- a/src/pre/simp-node-proof.c
+++ b/src/pre/simp-node-proof.c
@@ -49,7 +49,12 @@
     }
   else if (stack_size(DAGs) != DAG_arity(src))
     {
-      dest.DAG = DAG_dup(DAG_new_stack(DAG_symb(src), DAGs));
+      /* A one-argument `and`/`or` application is not well-formed Alethe, and printing one into
+         the proof (as the LHS of the `and_simplify`/`or_simplify` step that removes it again)
+         puts an ill-formed term in the output. When a single argument survives, it *is* the
+         result. */
+      dest.DAG = stack_size(DAGs) == 1 ? DAG_dup(stack_get(DAGs, 0))
+                                       : DAG_dup(DAG_new_stack(DAG_symb(src), DAGs));
       stack_INIT(dest.proof);
       stack_push(dest.proof, proof_transformation(type, src, dest.DAG, NULL));
       DAG_free(src);
@@ -94,7 +99,9 @@
         DAG_misc_set(PDAG[j], 0);
         j++;
       }
-  dest.DAG = DAG_dup(DAG_new(DAG_symb(src), j, PDAG));
+  /* As in `simplify_neutral_proof`: a single surviving argument is the result, not a
+     one-argument application of the connective. */
+  dest.DAG = j == 1 ? DAG_dup(PDAG[0]) : DAG_dup(DAG_new(DAG_symb(src), j, PDAG));
   stack_push(dest.proof, proof_transformation(type, src, dest.DAG, NULL));
   DAG_free(src);
   return dest;
